      "title": "Identifying Singular Matrices"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "a8326a72-42e4-509f-9c00-d12069c7c4a7",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $S$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $S$ is **not** a singular matrix\n- **II.** $S$ is a singular matrix\n- **III.** $det(S)=0$\n\n$$\nS=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{7} & 0 & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & -2 & -3 \\\\ - 2 \\sqrt{7} & -2 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(S) & =|\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{7} & 0 & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & -2 & -3 \\\\ - 2 \\sqrt{7} & -2 & -4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{7}|\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 \\\\ -2 & -4\\end{aligned}|+0|\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 \\\\ - 2 \\sqrt{7} & -4\\end{aligned}|+\\frac{1}{2}|\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ - 2 \\sqrt{7} & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{7}(8-6)+0+\\frac{1}{2}(0- 4 \\sqrt{7}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{7}\\cdot 2+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (- 4 \\sqrt{7}) \\\\  & =2 \\sqrt{7}- 2 \\sqrt{7} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(S)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statements II and III are true.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "7b0e997c-6339-56a3-a454-89775a19868e",
          ":answer-field/key": "I",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/checkbox",
