    "question": {
      ":question/id": "5411f724-435a-5ea7-b982-536e38746e59",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $N$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $N$ is **not** a singular matrix\n\n- **II.** $N$ is a singular matrix\n\n- **III.** $det(N)=\\frac{8}{5} + \\frac{8 \\sqrt{2}}{5}$\n\n$$\nN=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & \\sqrt{2} & 1 \\\\ - \\frac{2}{5} & 0 & -1 \\\\ 4 & \\sqrt{2} & 6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(N) & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & \\sqrt{2} & 1 \\\\ - \\frac{2}{5} & 0 & -1 \\\\ 4 & \\sqrt{2} & 6\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2|\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & 6\\end{aligned}|+(- \\sqrt{2})|\\begin{aligned}- \\frac{2}{5} & -1 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{aligned}|+1|\\begin{aligned}- \\frac{2}{5} & 0 \\\\ 4 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2(0+\\sqrt{2})+(- \\sqrt{2})(- \\frac{12}{5}+4)+1(- \\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{5}+0) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\sqrt{2}+(- \\sqrt{2})\\cdot \\frac{8}{5}+(- \\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{5}+0) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(N)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statement II is true.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "21102572-4693-5307-ac96-5a555e0eada9",
          ":answer-field/key": "I",
