  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186059848,
      "id": "32df8909-a239-5167-b8ea-412f7cdaea93",
      "title": "Identifying Singular Matrices"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "dece81e9-f071-5fed-ac37-29d7339040bd",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $P$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $P$ is **not** a singular matrix\n\n- **II.** $P$ is a singular matrix\n\n- **III.** $det(P)=0$\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{3}{2} & - \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 0 & -3 & 4 \\\\ - \\frac{1}{5} & - \\frac{9}{5} & \\frac{11}{5}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(P) & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{3}{2} & - \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 0 & -3 & 4 \\\\ - \\frac{1}{5} & - \\frac{9}{5} & \\frac{11}{5}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}|\\begin{aligned}-3 & 4 \\\\ - \\frac{9}{5} & \\frac{11}{5}\\end{aligned}|+(- \\frac{3}{2})|\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ - \\frac{1}{5} & \\frac{11}{5}\\end{aligned}|+(- \\frac{3}{2})|\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 \\\\ - \\frac{1}{5} & - \\frac{9}{5}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(- \\frac{33}{5}+\\frac{36}{5})+(- \\frac{3}{2})(0+\\frac{4}{5})+(- \\frac{3}{2})(0- \\frac{3}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{5}+(- \\frac{3}{2})\\cdot \\frac{4}{5}+(- \\frac{3}{2})\\cdot (- \\frac{3}{5}) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(P)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statements II and III are true.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "4f961c20-149b-579c-959c-bf23ebfb865b",
          ":answer-field/key": "I",
