    "question": {
      ":question/id": "5704987e-d401-587d-a874-f1b5b23ee666",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $F$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $det(F)=- \\frac{4 \\sqrt{3}}{3}$\n- **II.** $det(F)=8 \\sqrt{3}$\n- **III.** $F$ is a singular matrix\n\n$$\nF=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & \\sqrt{3} & -2 \\\\ - \\frac{2}{3} & 0 & 2 \\\\ -5 & \\sqrt{3} & 8\\end{bmatrix}\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(F) & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\sqrt{3} & -2 \\\\ - \\frac{2}{3} & 0 & 2 \\\\ -5 & \\sqrt{3} & 8\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-1)|\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & 8\\end{aligned}|+(- \\sqrt{3})|\\begin{aligned}- \\frac{2}{3} & 2 \\\\ -5 & 8\\end{aligned}|+(-2)|\\begin{aligned}- \\frac{2}{3} & 0 \\\\ -5 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-1)(0- 2 \\sqrt{3})+(- \\sqrt{3})(- \\frac{16}{3}+10)+(-2)(- \\frac{2 \\sqrt{3}}{3}+0) \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot (- 2 \\sqrt{3})+(- \\sqrt{3})\\cdot \\frac{14}{3}+(-2)\\cdot (- \\frac{2 \\sqrt{3}}{3}) \\\\  & =2 \\sqrt{3}- \\frac{14 \\sqrt{3}}{3}+\\frac{4 \\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\  & =- \\frac{4 \\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(F)=- \\frac{4 \\sqrt{3}}{3}\\ne 0$, the matrix is **not** singular. \n\nTherefore, only statement I is true.",
