      ":question/id": "b95e53dd-35a0-50c9-bfff-11523349252e",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $S$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $det(S)=0$\n- **II.** $det(S)=-1$\n- **III.** $S$ is a singular matrix\n\n$$\nS=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & \\sqrt{2} & -2 \\\\ - \\frac{1}{2} & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{2} & -4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(S) & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\sqrt{2} & -2 \\\\ - \\frac{1}{2} & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{2} & -4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-1)|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & -4\\end{aligned}|+(- \\sqrt{2})|\\begin{aligned}- \\frac{1}{2} & 1 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}|+(-2)|\\begin{aligned}- \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-1)(0- \\sqrt{2})+(- \\sqrt{2})(2+0)+(-2)(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+0) \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot (- \\sqrt{2})+(- \\sqrt{2})\\cdot 2+(-2)\\cdot (- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}- 2 \\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{2} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(S)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statements I and III are true.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "85e83677-edaa-5314-a7a2-d524633952d2",
          ":answer-field/key": "I",
