    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "b57fbb9f-ad10-5cb0-952d-1ee0590f2626",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $N$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $N$ is **not** a singular matrix\n\n- **II.** $N$ is a singular matrix\n\n- **III.** $det(N)=- \\frac{22}{5}$\n\n$$\nN=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 & -2 \\\\ -3 & -2 & -1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{5} & \\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{3}{5}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(N) & =|\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & -2 \\\\ -3 & -2 & -1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{5} & \\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-3)|\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}|+0|\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{5} & \\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}|+(-2)|\\begin{aligned}-3 & -2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{5} & \\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-3)(- \\frac{6}{5}+\\frac{3}{5})+0+(-2)(- \\frac{9}{5}+\\frac{2}{5}) \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot (- \\frac{3}{5})+(-2)\\cdot (- \\frac{7}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{23}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(N)=\\frac{23}{5}\\ne 0$, the matrix is **not** singular. \n\nTherefore, only statement I is true.",
