    "question": {
      ":question/id": "29afae0e-f4ff-5b17-8a87-023c938c5332",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $M$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $M$ is a singular matrix\n- **II.** $M$ is **not** a singular matrix\n- **III.** $det(M)=\\frac{48}{5}$\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2} & 0 & - \\frac{1}{5} \\\\ - 2 \\sqrt{2} & -2 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 2 \\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =|\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} & 0 & - \\frac{1}{5} \\\\ - 2 \\sqrt{2} & -2 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 2 \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}|+0|\\begin{aligned}- 2 \\sqrt{2} & 0 \\\\ -4 & 2 \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}|+(- \\frac{1}{5})|\\begin{aligned}- 2 \\sqrt{2} & -2 \\\\ -4 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}(- 4 \\sqrt{2}+0)+0+(- \\frac{1}{5})(0-8) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot (- 4 \\sqrt{2})+(- \\frac{1}{5})\\cdot (-8) \\\\  & =-8+\\frac{8}{5} \\\\  & =- \\frac{32}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(M)=- \\frac{32}{5}\\ne 0$, the matrix is **not** singular. \n\nTherefore, only statement II is true.",
