    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "ae45e256-addb-5dde-a937-ddaf2183d968",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $P$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $det(P)=-8$\n- **II.** $det(P)=- \\frac{32}{3}$\n- **III.** $P$ is a singular matrix\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -1 & \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -3 & - \\frac{3}{2} & - \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ -6 & -2 & \\frac{4}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(P) & =|\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 & \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -3 & - \\frac{3}{2} & - \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ -6 & -2 & \\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-3)|\\begin{aligned}- \\frac{3}{2} & - \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ -2 & \\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}|+1|\\begin{aligned}-3 & - \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ -6 & \\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}|+\\frac{2}{3}|\\begin{aligned}-3 & - \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -6 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-3)(-2- \\frac{2}{3})+1(-4-2)+\\frac{2}{3}(6-9) \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot (- \\frac{8}{3})+1\\cdot (-6)+\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =8-6-2 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(P)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statement III is true.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "35bc12bc-e397-579d-a860-03a23edb40ba",
          ":answer-field/key": "I",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/checkbox",
