    "question": {
      ":question/id": "1e0566f2-2b89-561b-80e1-85f97a27652b",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $E$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $E$ is **not** a singular matrix\n- **II.** $E$ is a singular matrix\n- **III.** $det(E)=- \\frac{32}{21}$\n\n$$\nE=\\begin{bmatrix}- \\frac{1}{7} & - \\frac{1}{7} & - \\frac{2}{7} \\\\ \\frac{4}{3} & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(E) & =|\\begin{aligned}- \\frac{1}{7} & - \\frac{1}{7} & - \\frac{2}{7} \\\\ \\frac{4}{3} & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(- \\frac{1}{7})|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}|+\\frac{1}{7}|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{3} & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}|+(- \\frac{2}{7})|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{3} & 0 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(- \\frac{1}{7})(0+0)+\\frac{1}{7}(0+0)+(- \\frac{2}{7})(\\frac{16}{3}+0) \\\\  & =(- \\frac{1}{7})\\cdot 0+\\frac{1}{7}\\cdot 0+(- \\frac{2}{7})\\cdot \\frac{16}{3} \\\\  & =- \\frac{32}{21} \\\\  & =- \\frac{32}{21}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(E)=- \\frac{32}{21}\\ne 0$, the matrix is **not** singular. \n\nTherefore, only statements I and III are true.",
